<BaseLayout title="Environmental Justice & Industrial Hog Farming" description="A collaborative North Carolina Summer Policy Institute analysis of industrial hog farming, environmental exposure, land use, and policy options.">
<article class="hog"><header><div class="container"><a class="back" href="/research-writing/">← Research &amp; Writing</a><div class="eyebrow">Environmental policy analysis / Historical project</div><h1>Environmental justice<br/><em>in eastern North Carolina.</em></h1><p class="deck">Industrial hog farming, community exposure, and the policy choices surrounding agricultural production.</p><p class="meta">North Carolina Summer Policy Institute · Collaborative research · 2018</p></div></header>
<section class="intro"><div class="container split"><div><span class="marker">01 / The question</span><h2>Who bears the environmental costs of production?</h2></div><div><p>Industrial livestock production supports a major agricultural economy, but concentrated operations also create challenges involving waste management, air and water quality, and the communities living nearby.</p><p>At the North Carolina Summer Policy Institute, I worked with Jenna Nabors, Kahlia Phillips, and Joshua White on a collaborative presentation examining environmental justice concerns associated with hog farms in eastern North Carolina.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="exposure"><div class="container"><span class="marker">02 / Exposure pathways</span><h2>From production to surrounding communities.</h2><div class="path"><div><strong>Concentrated operations</strong><p>Large numbers of animals produce substantial amounts of waste that must be stored, treated, and managed.</p></div><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><div><strong>Storage &amp; land application</strong><p>The project examined waste lagoons and application to agricultural land as potential sources of emissions and runoff.</p></div><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><div><strong>Environmental concerns</strong><p>Nearby residents may experience odors, air-quality impacts, and risks related to water contamination.</p></div></div><p class="source">Conceptual pathways summarized from the original team presentation; this is not a measurement of exposure at any specific property.</p></div></section>
<section class="questions"><div class="container split"><div><span class="marker">03 / Environmental justice</span><h2>Location matters.</h2></div><div><p>Our analysis considered concerns about the proximity of industrial hog operations to lower-income communities and communities of color. Beyond the environmental mechanisms, we examined how industrial land-use decisions can distribute risks unevenly.</p><p>The policy question was not only how waste is handled, but also how affected communities are protected and how decisions are made.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="options"><div class="container"><span class="marker">04 / Policy options considered</span><h2>Possible interventions—and their trade-offs.</h2><div class="option-list"><div><span>01</span><h3>Waste infrastructure</h3><p>Explore treatment improvements and possible waste-to-resource technologies, weighing environmental benefits against costs and implementation capacity.</p></div><div><span>02</span><h3>Land use &amp; setbacks</h3><p>Consider minimum distances between waste operations and residences, including the practical constraints for existing farms.</p></div><div><span>03</span><h3>Production &amp; oversight</h3><p>Examine limits on facility concentration and stronger standards, recognizing enforcement demands and the economic role of agriculture.</p></div><div><span>04</span><h3>Community protection</h3><p>Consider compensation, health-related assistance, and remediation alongside measures intended to prevent future harm.</p></div></div></div></section>
<section class="close"><div class="container split"><div><span class="marker">05 / Reflection</span><h2>Policy lives in the details of implementation.</h2></div><div><p>Our presentation weighed environmental and community concerns against regulatory processes, existing legislation, and the industry's economic importance. The exercise connected environmental research to the practical constraints of public decision-making.</p><p>It remains part of my interest in land use, resource allocation, environmental data, and the ways policy decisions affect communities.</p><p class="archive">This is a historical collaborative policy exercise, not a current regulatory review. Proposed interventions were exploratory and were not implemented or evaluated by our team. Original presentation: Jenna Nabors, Avery Moon, Kahlia Phillips, and Joshua White.</p><p class="program">Program context: <a href="https://ncbudget.org/ncspi/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">North Carolina Summer Policy Institute, NC Budget &amp; Tax Center ↗</a></p></div></div></section>
